CRITERION 3 -- (c) the scan is scoped and cannot misfire.       PASS
==================================================================
(a) THE HELPER STRIPS CORRECTLY, and the phrase really is in the live body.
Probe zz_probe_test.go in .../b2-c3, no-overlap fixture, GET /accounts:
  whole-body contains "Pattern overlap warning" = true
  stripped   contains "Pattern overlap warning" = false
  script tag count in body = 9   ->  stripped script tag count = 0
All 9 script elements the page actually serves are removed.

(b) THE SCOPING IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. In .../b2-c3c I un-scoped the
assertion back to the whole body, changing nothing else:
    rendered := renderedBodyWithoutScripts(w.Body.String())  ->  rendered := w.Body.String()
$ go test -count=1 -run TestHandlePage_RendererMode_NoOverlapRendersNoWarningBlock ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  --- FAIL      C3_LOADBEARING_EXIT=1
So with the new consistent wording ("Pattern overlap warning dismissed.") the
old whole-body scan WOULD misfire, and the helper is what prevents it. That is
exactly the coupling the S5 row says (c) exists to break.

(c) NON-VACUITY. In .../b2-c3b I forced a warning onto the no-overlap page
(handlers.go:600, `warnings = append(warnings, "MUTANT: spurious overlap")`):
$ go test -count=1 -run TestHandlePage_RendererMode_NoOverlapRendersNoWarningBlock ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  --- FAIL: ... handlers_test.go:533: non-overlapping config rendered the
            overlap warning block      NONVAC_EXIT=1
The test still detects a genuinely rendered warning block.

(d) REGEX EDGE CASES, `(?s)<script[^>]*>.*?</script>` (table probe, b2-c3):
  OK   plain                    A<script>X</script>B              -> AB
  OK   attrs                    <script src="/x.js" defer>        -> AB
  OK   attr-with-gt-in-value    <script data-q="a>b">             -> AB
  OK   two-blocks-keeps-between A<script>X</script>MID<script>Y</script>B -> AMIDB
  OK   type-attr                <script type="application/json">  -> AB
  OK   newlines                 multi-line body                   -> AB
  OK   unclosed                 A<script>X                        -> unchanged
  DIFF uppercase                A<SCRIPT>X</SCRIPT>B              -> NOT stripped
  DIFF close-in-js-string       <script>var s="</script>";</script>B -> leaves `";`
The non-greedy `.*?` is what matters: the two-blocks case keeps MID, so the
helper cannot OVER-strip across separate script elements and cannot make the
test vacuously green. Both DIFF rows are UNDER-strips, whose only consequence
is a false FAILURE, never a false pass -- and the second one is not even a
bug, since an HTML parser also ends a script element at the first `</script>`
inside a JS string. Uppercase `<SCRIPT>` occurs nowhere in web/templates
(`grep -rn "<script" web/templates/` -> 20 hits, all lowercase). Nested
<script> is not expressible in HTML. Criterion met; the two under-strips are
recorded as an observation, not a finding.

Cross-check that (c)'s reword did not break the sibling "renders once" test:
that test counts `">Pattern overlap warning<"` (handlers_test.go:567), a form
the JS string cannot match, and it passes (see criterion 6).

